1
00:00:00,386 --> 00:00:01,986
Meet Frosty Fusion.

2
00:00:02,243 --> 00:00:05,357
A little ice cream shop with
a very loyal following.

3
00:00:05,643 --> 00:00:07,157
Five years of scooping.

4
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:08,671
Forty flavors.

5
00:00:08,843 --> 00:00:12,243
And one strawberry that
people drive across town for.

6
00:00:12,443 --> 00:00:16,814
Now, type "best strawberry ice cream"
into Google, anywhere near their shop…

7
00:00:16,986 --> 00:00:18,043
…and there they are.

8
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:19,129
Right at the top.

9
00:00:19,455 --> 00:00:21,536
That spot didn't happen by luck.

10
00:00:21,736 --> 00:00:23,329
It's SEO.

11
00:00:23,379 --> 00:00:28,800
SEO, search engine optimization, is
everything that helps Google decide

12
00:00:28,850 --> 00:00:31,507
your page is the best answer to a search.

13
00:00:31,822 --> 00:00:34,850
For Frosty Fusion,
that's five years of it.

14
00:00:35,193 --> 00:00:36,793
Pages Google trusts.

15
00:00:37,022 --> 00:00:39,250
Food blogs that linked
to their strawberry.

16
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:41,679
Reviews that mention them by name.

17
00:00:42,022 --> 00:00:46,907
And all of it is tied to one
address: getfrostyfusion.com.

18
00:00:47,472 --> 00:00:49,499
There was one small problem.

19
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:53,871
Customers kept asking if Frosty
Fusion was a smoothie bar.

20
00:00:54,128 --> 00:00:57,471
One ordered a green detox. Fair.

21
00:00:57,699 --> 00:01:00,642
"Fusion" does sound a little… blended.

22
00:01:00,813 --> 00:01:02,328
So, a new name.

23
00:01:02,413 --> 00:01:03,699
Frosty Bites.

24
00:01:03,785 --> 00:01:06,442
Short, sweet, and nobody's ordering kale.

25
00:01:06,728 --> 00:01:09,442
New domain: getfrostybites.com.

26
00:01:09,768 --> 00:01:13,511
And then comes the thought that keeps
every business owner up at night.

27
00:01:13,797 --> 00:01:15,711
What happens to the Google spot?

28
00:01:15,997 --> 00:01:17,282
The strawberry page.

29
00:01:17,597 --> 00:01:18,882
The five years.

30
00:01:19,225 --> 00:01:23,632
Change the domain the wrong way, and
Google meets Frosty Bites like a total

31
00:01:23,682 --> 00:01:27,775
stranger. Do it right, and Google
can carry your rankings over to

32
00:01:27,825 --> 00:01:30,425
the new name. You don't start from zero.

33
00:01:30,768 --> 00:01:34,975
So how do you change your
domain name without losing SEO?

34
00:01:35,025 --> 00:01:36,939
It comes down to three steps.

35
00:01:37,139 --> 00:01:40,597
But before the steps, one
thing most people get wrong.

36
00:01:41,265 --> 00:01:43,208
Google doesn't rank your domain.

37
00:01:43,437 --> 00:01:44,808
It ranks your pages.

38
00:01:45,094 --> 00:01:49,608
To Google, Frosty Fusion is a
list of addresses. The homepage.

39
00:01:49,779 --> 00:01:50,779
The flavor menu.

40
00:01:51,122 --> 00:01:56,779
And that famous page:
getfrostyfusion.com/flavors/strawberry.

41
00:01:57,122 --> 00:01:59,265
Each page has its own history.

42
00:01:59,522 --> 00:02:01,951
How long it has ranked,
and who links to it.

43
00:02:02,237 --> 00:02:05,701
Change the domain, and every
one of those addresses changes

44
00:02:05,751 --> 00:02:08,808
too. The new ones start with no history.

45
00:02:09,122 --> 00:02:11,408
So the job isn't moving a domain.

46
00:02:11,551 --> 00:02:14,979
It's moving every page, in
a way Google can follow.

47
00:02:15,294 --> 00:02:19,958
So how do you move every page, and
keep everything it has earned?

48
00:02:20,008 --> 00:02:24,579
Three steps. Map it: match every
old address to its new one.

49
00:02:24,865 --> 00:02:29,379
Redirect it: send each old address
to its match, permanently.

50
00:02:29,722 --> 00:02:32,808
And tell Google, so it knows
the whole site has moved.

51
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:34,885
Step one: the map.

52
00:02:35,171 --> 00:02:38,142
Old address on the left,
new address on the right.

53
00:02:38,428 --> 00:02:47,428
getfrostyfusion.com/flavors/strawberry becomes
getfrostybites.com/flavors/strawberry.

54
00:02:47,713 --> 00:02:51,571
If the paths stay the same,
one rule covers every page.

55
00:02:51,856 --> 00:02:55,171
If some pages changed, each
one gets its own line.

56
00:02:55,456 --> 00:03:00,721
Quick tip: before you change anything,
export your page list from your sitemap or

57
00:03:00,771 --> 00:03:02,228
from Google Search Console.

58
00:03:02,456 --> 00:03:03,828
That's your inventory.

59
00:03:04,214 --> 00:03:08,043
Now, here's where most domain
changes quietly go wrong.

60
00:03:08,329 --> 00:03:10,871
Mapping every page feels like a chore.

61
00:03:11,043 --> 00:03:15,271
So people take a shortcut: send
everything to the new homepage.

62
00:03:15,471 --> 00:03:16,786
And it looks perfect.

63
00:03:16,957 --> 00:03:19,671
Every old link lands somewhere. No errors.

64
00:03:19,843 --> 00:03:22,986
Strawberry fans arrive
at getfrostybites.com.

65
00:03:23,357 --> 00:03:25,300
But they wanted the strawberry.

66
00:03:25,557 --> 00:03:27,757
Now they're on a homepage, hunting for it.

67
00:03:28,043 --> 00:03:29,671
And Google notices too.

68
00:03:29,814 --> 00:03:32,443
Hundreds of pages, all poured into one.

69
00:03:32,729 --> 00:03:35,471
It can treat them as pages
that no longer exist.

70
00:03:35,757 --> 00:03:38,443
And that top spot for "best
strawberry ice cream"?

71
00:03:38,729 --> 00:03:39,871
It starts to melt.

72
00:03:41,129 --> 00:03:44,871
One old page, one matching
new page. That's the rule.

73
00:03:45,422 --> 00:03:47,422
Step two: the redirects.

74
00:03:47,736 --> 00:03:51,477
Here's what that looks like
in RedirHub. Links. Create.

75
00:03:51,763 --> 00:03:52,991
Website Migration.

76
00:03:53,191 --> 00:03:56,134
Old domain: getfrostyfusion.com.

77
00:03:56,420 --> 00:04:00,247
New domain: getfrostybites.com.
Same paths?

78
00:04:00,505 --> 00:04:04,190
One same-path rule, and every
page forwards to its twin.

79
00:04:04,476 --> 00:04:05,476
Changed pages?

80
00:04:05,762 --> 00:04:09,619
Paste your map, or upload it
as a spreadsheet file, a CSV.

81
00:04:09,962 --> 00:04:13,912
Before anything goes live, RedirHub
shows you what it will create,

82
00:04:13,962 --> 00:04:16,162
and flags every row with a problem.

83
00:04:16,476 --> 00:04:19,247
Nothing is created until you click Create.

84
00:04:19,533 --> 00:04:22,905
Every redirect should be a
301, the permanent kind.

85
00:04:23,247 --> 00:04:27,590
It's the signal Google uses to carry
a page's history to its new address.

86
00:04:27,876 --> 00:04:32,819
Then point getfrostyfusion.com at
RedirHub with the records it gives you.

87
00:04:33,133 --> 00:04:35,019
The padlock is set up automatically.

88
00:04:35,162 --> 00:04:36,819
Our earlier videos walk through it.

89
00:04:37,205 --> 00:04:39,719
Step three: tell Google you've moved.

90
00:04:40,062 --> 00:04:43,634
Google Search Console is Google's
free dashboard for your site.

91
00:04:43,919 --> 00:04:47,584
Open the old site there, and use
Change of Address to point it at

92
00:04:47,634 --> 00:04:52,834
the new one. Then submit the new sitemap:
the list of pages you want Google to find.

93
00:04:53,119 --> 00:04:55,319
And update everything you control.

94
00:04:55,519 --> 00:04:56,755
Your site's own links.

95
00:04:56,805 --> 00:04:58,205
Your Instagram bio.

96
00:04:58,262 --> 00:04:59,891
The QR code on the menu.

97
00:05:00,062 --> 00:05:01,405
Your Google Maps listing.

98
00:05:01,691 --> 00:05:03,777
All of it says Frosty Bites now.

99
00:05:04,152 --> 00:05:06,695
Now, test it the way Google sees it.

100
00:05:07,009 --> 00:05:11,580
Open RedirHub's free Redirect Checker,
and paste the old strawberry page.

101
00:05:11,752 --> 00:05:14,187
One hop. A 301.

102
00:05:14,237 --> 00:05:18,980
And it lands on
getfrostybites.com/flavors/strawberry.

103
00:05:19,209 --> 00:05:21,480
That's a pass. Try a few more.

104
00:05:21,823 --> 00:05:22,937
Your best sellers.

105
00:05:23,195 --> 00:05:24,566
An old blog post.

106
00:05:24,737 --> 00:05:26,595
The www version.

107
00:05:27,149 --> 00:05:28,692
And here's the sweet part.

108
00:05:28,978 --> 00:05:33,384
A regular finds a menu from 2021,
the one that still says Frosty

109
00:05:33,434 --> 00:05:35,291
Fusion. She scans it…

110
00:05:35,434 --> 00:05:37,520
and lands right on the strawberry.

111
00:05:37,862 --> 00:05:41,405
Every old link, every
old flyer, still works.

112
00:05:42,286 --> 00:05:45,055
Then comes the part
nobody warns you about.

113
00:05:45,311 --> 00:05:48,025
For a few weeks, your rankings may wobble.

114
00:05:48,311 --> 00:05:51,968
That's Google re-reading your site
at its new address. It's normal.

115
00:05:52,197 --> 00:05:54,997
Whatever you do, don't undo the redirects.

116
00:05:55,339 --> 00:05:59,204
And RedirHub's analytics show how many
people still come in through the old

117
00:05:59,254 --> 00:06:01,482
domain, so you can watch the move happen.

118
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:03,383
Two rules before you go.

119
00:06:03,669 --> 00:06:06,240
Keep the redirects for at least a year.

120
00:06:06,497 --> 00:06:09,583
Google says as long as possible. Honestly?

121
00:06:09,754 --> 00:06:10,897
Keep them for good.

122
00:06:11,154 --> 00:06:14,097
And keep renewing getfrostyfusion.com.

123
00:06:14,383 --> 00:06:16,783
If it expires, someone else can buy it,

124
00:06:16,926 --> 00:06:19,383
along with every link
that still points to it.

125
00:06:19,612 --> 00:06:22,697
And every strawberry fan who types it. So.

126
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:24,554
Map every page.

127
00:06:24,754 --> 00:06:29,069
Redirect each one to its twin
with a 301. Tell Google.

128
00:06:29,326 --> 00:06:30,812
Then let it settle.

129
00:06:31,182 --> 00:06:34,668
A few weeks later, search "best
strawberry ice cream" again…

130
00:06:34,868 --> 00:06:36,411
…and there's Frosty Bites.

131
00:06:36,525 --> 00:06:38,696
Right where Frosty Fusion used to be.

132
00:06:39,275 --> 00:06:41,075
Same ice cream. New name.

133
00:06:41,246 --> 00:06:42,532
Same spot on Google.

134
00:06:42,818 --> 00:06:45,675
Links to everything in this
video are in the description.

135
00:06:45,818 --> 00:06:47,589
And if you get stuck, reach out.

136
00:06:47,675 --> 00:06:48,818
We're happy to help.

137
00:06:49,046 --> 00:06:54,161
Next week: moving hundreds of domains
at once, with one spreadsheet.
